WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.440
7 billion light years away, a supercluster of two quadrillion suns may show if dark matter interacts. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 225. El Gordo, as the cluster is nicknamed, contains two colliding regions that are still merging. Fizz.org reports that the object is unusually large for such an early stretch of cosmic time. Light from more distant galaxies, bent as it passes the cluster, is what supplied the mass map. Those two cores, still in the act of coming to

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.760
together, give a working laboratory for dark matter physics at cluster scales.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.760
The same maps show where the visible galaxies sit compared with the peak of the total mass.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.880
Picture that pile of mass packed into a span like the distance from New York to Chicago,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.900
repeated only a few times.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.260
The observation catches the universe at less than half the age it has now.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.700
Two dense cores are still slamming together, and that kind of collision should have flung ordinary

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.840
matter apart, unless something unseen kept the gravity locked. If dark matter particles

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.440
slid through each other without exchanging any momentum, those cores would have pulled apart,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.240
and the whole structure would have come undone. The distribution stays tightly bound instead,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:17.300
which implies the particles do interact when they pass close. That scattering slows the cores as

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.560
they try to separate, and gravity then rakes in extra material faster than ordinary cold dark

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.860
matter computer runs allow. A giant cluster therefore appears earlier than most of those runs

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:33.260
predict for that epoch. The maps place the dark matter cores offset from the shining galaxies,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.020
a pattern that matching simulations reproduce only when a small self-interaction cross-section is

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:45.200
switched on. How far that offset runs and the fact the cores remain bound at all, together set a

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.040
bound on how strongly the particles must scatter. Nobody yet knows the exact strength and no laboratory

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.560
detector has recorded a particle that behaves this way at the densities found in clusters.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.980
The live question is whether the same interaction also fits every other massive cluster seen

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.460
at similar red shifts, or whether El Gordo is an outlier.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.400
An outlier would force a rewrite of how fast the first giant structures were allowed to grow

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.360
after the Big Bang.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:16.560
Deeper lensing maps of more high redshift clusters, plus simulations that follow particle scattering

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.720
across cosmic time, are what it will take to settle that.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:27.135
A University of Missouri astronomer now heads a team of 130 researchers drawn from 62 institutions.

00:02:27.335 --> 00:02:32.555
The Fizz.org announcement names the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA.

00:02:33.155 --> 00:02:38.355
The California Institute of Technology, Princeton University, and Harvard University among them.

00:02:38.975 --> 00:02:43.575
The mission is built to capture a view of the early universe that earlier campaigns did not reach.

00:02:43.575 --> 00:02:48.415
expertise from those universities and from NASA centers is being pooled to push

00:02:48.415 --> 00:02:53.255
observations of cosmic dawn past what prior surveys achieved. The group will

00:02:53.255 --> 00:02:58.075
coordinate the observing and the data analysis across dozens of sites. The aim is

00:02:58.075 --> 00:03:02.943
one unified picture of the first galaxies and the environments around them. Hubble

00:03:02.943 --> 00:03:10.103
recorded an edge-on portrait of the intermediate spiral NGC-1827. That galaxy lies

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:16.723
roughly 37 million light-years away in Columbus. Fizz.org notes. Bright blue star-forming regions

00:03:16.723 --> 00:03:23.163
line the disk and dark dust lanes cut across it. The frame belongs to a survey aimed at 260 satellite

00:03:23.163 --> 00:03:30.383
galaxies around 22 Milky Way-like hosts inside 32 million light-years. 111 of those satellites have

00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:37.023
been observed so far, with 149 orbits still to fly. The advanced camera for surveys took the data

00:03:37.023 --> 00:03:41.363
and a foreground star with diffraction spikes sits between Earth and the target.

00:03:42.103 --> 00:03:46.903
Low-mass companions in the sample are the test of cosmological predictions on small scales,

00:03:47.483 --> 00:03:50.703
and finishing the faint companion census will take more Hubble time.

00:03:51.654 --> 00:03:56.694
Blue origin is putting money into four launch pads, three landing ships, and a line that

00:03:56.694 --> 00:03:59.554
can turn out up to 150 upper stages a year.

00:04:00.314 --> 00:04:05.194
Ars Technica reports that this capacity is size for high-flight volume on the New Glenn rocket.

00:04:05.194 --> 00:04:09.934
it. The spending marks a turn from development work toward a sustained operations tempo.

00:04:10.594 --> 00:04:15.514
Those three landing ships are there to support rapid reuse of returning hardware. Ground

00:04:15.514 --> 00:04:20.274
systems are being built for several vehicles moving and recovering at once, so new Glenn

00:04:20.274 --> 00:04:25.892
can fly off and once the cadence is real. Plants started changing their gene activity almost

00:04:25.892 --> 00:04:31.292
as soon as they left Earth on a University of Florida suborbital experiment. The work published

00:04:31.292 --> 00:04:36.752
in the journal NPJ Microgravity and covered by Fizz.org, flew on Blue Origins

00:04:36.752 --> 00:04:43.472
New Shepherd on August 29, 2024. Gene activity shifted within minutes of microgravity, which

00:04:43.472 --> 00:04:48.912
is the first fine-grained timeline of that molecular turn. The run followed several gene pathways

00:04:48.912 --> 00:04:53.412
at once to see which ones switch on first when Earth gravity gives way to weightlessness.

00:04:54.072 --> 00:04:58.812
Plant cells, in other words, begin sensing the new environment on a clock measured in minutes,

00:04:58.812 --> 00:04:59.732
estimates, not ours.

00:05:01.094 --> 00:05:05.774
Astronomers combine archival files with new radial velocity measurements to hunt for planets

00:05:05.774 --> 00:05:08.394
around the nearby binary 70-OF AB.

00:05:09.194 --> 00:05:14.274
Universe today reports that no planets turned up, yet Jupiter mass worlds are now ruled

00:05:14.274 --> 00:05:16.074
out at key distances from the pair.

00:05:16.714 --> 00:05:19.734
Smaller Earth-sized planets are still allowed by those limits.

00:05:20.534 --> 00:05:24.174
Decades of older velocities plus the fresh data are what tightened the box.

00:05:24.174 --> 00:05:28.974
The merge set also sharpens the orbital solution for the two stars themselves.

00:05:30.036 --> 00:05:34.896
Future telescope time can now go to the separations where Earth mass worlds remain possible,

00:05:35.356 --> 00:05:37.036
instead of sweeping the whole range.

00:05:37.696 --> 00:05:42.716
Ruling out certain masses at certain distances is a real update to the catalog of nearby stars,

00:05:43.116 --> 00:05:47.616
and the cleaner binary orbit cuts uncertainty for later searches around similar pairs.

00:05:47.616 --> 00:05:53.116
Doctors, Dr. Brant Gatches at the University of Duisburg, S.N., led an international team

00:05:53.116 --> 00:05:56.856
that built a high-resolution three-dimensional model of stellar nurseries.

00:05:57.436 --> 00:06:02.096
Uneven cloud structure in that model throws shadows that rewrite local chemistry and

00:06:02.096 --> 00:06:07.396
change the infrared glow recorded by the James Webb Space Telescope, or JWST.

00:06:07.876 --> 00:06:12.476
The paper is an astronomy and astrophysics, with Fizz.org carrying the account.

00:06:12.476 --> 00:06:18.616
dense filaments are the shadowcasters, and they shift the mix of molecules JWST actually sees.

00:06:19.256 --> 00:06:24.176
The simulation keeps both the cloud geometry and the chemical gradients at scales that older

00:06:24.176 --> 00:06:29.656
averaged models had smoothed the way. Synthetic spectra from the run were checked directly against

00:06:29.656 --> 00:06:36.881
real JWST spectra to confirm that the shadow-driven chemistry holds. The commander of SpaceX's

00:06:36.881 --> 00:06:41.761
crew-12 mission described how the view of Earth from orbit changes a person's sense of how fragile

00:06:41.761 --> 00:06:46.741
all the planet is. Space.com reports that the astronauts said they wished more people

00:06:46.741 --> 00:06:51.801
could see the world as a small outpost of life. The remarks came after their recent return

00:06:51.801 --> 00:06:56.381
from the International Space Station. Four crew members had lived aboard the station

00:06:56.381 --> 00:07:01.501
for an extended stay before splashdown. From that altitude, they spoke of a thin atmosphere

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:06.581
and of how few zones can actually hold life. Against the black of space, they said,

00:07:06.581 --> 00:07:09.041
The biosphere looks isolated and delicate.

00:07:09.441 --> 00:07:15.374
Before we go, watch the Missouri-led Early Universe Campaign as it begins knitting observations

00:07:15.374 --> 00:07:18.594
from dozens of sites into one map of the first galaxies.

00:07:19.114 --> 00:07:21.554
That covers today's Space and Science news.

